<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Data Protection, at the strictest class.

	  This does NOT touch the API token: that lives in the Keychain, governed
	  by the Keychain and not by this entitlement. By default it is
	  kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly with no access control -
	  unreadable while locked, never backed up, but readable without a prompt
	  whenever the device is unlocked. When the operator turns on "Require
	  Face ID to open" for a server, it is
	  kSecAttrAccessibleWhenPasscodeSetThisDeviceOnly plus a SecAccessControl
	  (biometrics, or the passcode), the strongest protection iOS offers.

	  What it does cover is every file the app container holds — in practice the
	  UserDefaults plist carrying the configured servers: internal host names,
	  ports and the operator's labels for them. Without this they sit at the iOS
	  default of NSFileProtectionCompleteUntilFirstUserAuthentication, meaning
	  readable from the moment the device is first unlocked after boot, which is
	  effectively always. With it they are readable only while the device is
	  unlocked. For a self-hosted DDI operator, a list of internal control-plane
	  host names is reconnaissance worth denying to a stolen, locked phone.

	  The cost, for whoever builds Phase 3: files at this class are NOT readable
	  while the device is locked, so a notification service extension or any
	  background launch must not assume UserDefaults can be read. The app has no
	  background execution today, and reads defaults only once the scene is
	  active.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.default-data-protection</key>
	<string>NSFileProtectionComplete</string>
</dict>
</plist>
